# Source: Slides "ICT3112 - 7 - Recepy for Loop Invariants - exercises", Exercise 4, and "tips a".
# The slides write length(A) and A[0] + ... + A[n - 1]; here sizeof(a) and sum(k, 0, n - 1, a[k]).
# The invariant adds n = sizeof(a) to the one of the slides, for the definedness of a[i].
id: array-sum
title: Sum of an array
topic: Arrays
level: 3
source: Slides 7, Recipe exercises, Exercise 4
tags: arrays, loop, invariant, sum, definedness, variant

task:
  The loop adds the elements of the array $a$ into $s$. We write
  $sum(k, 0, n - 1, a[k])$ for $a[0] + ... + a[n - 1]$. A sum whose upper
  bound is smaller than its lower bound is empty, and its value is $0$.

  Find a loop invariant $I$: $s$ is the sum of the first $i$ elements.
  Prove initialisation, preservation and the conclusion at the exit.
  The loop reads $a[i]$, so ARRAY-READ adds a definedness condition. Then
  give a variant and show that the loop terminates.

given:
  Loop condition B: $i < n$

pre: n = sizeof(a)
post: s = sum(k, 0, n - 1, a[k])

program:
  {P}
  {R2}
  i := 0;              // i1
  {R1}
  s := 0;              // i2
  {I}
  while (i < n) do {
    {I && i < n}
    {R4}
    s := s + a[i];     // b1
    {R3}
    i := i + 1         // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(i < n)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  At the loop head, positions $0$ to $i - 1$ have been visited and $s$ is
  their sum: $s = sum(k, 0, i - 1, a[k])$. This is $Q$ with $n$ replaced by
  the counter $i$. We add the bounds $0 <= i <= n$ of the counter, which
  the exit needs. The loop does not change $n$ or $a$, but preservation
  needs $n = sizeof(a)$ to show that the read $a[i]$ is inside the array,
  so we keep it from $P$.
set: I = s = sum(k, 0, i - 1, a[k]) && 0 <= i <= n && n = sizeof(a)
text:
  The loop rule with consequence has three premises, with the loop
  condition $B$ being $i < n$: initialisation (the statements before the
  loop establish $I$ from $P$), preservation $\{I && B\}$ body $\{I\}$,
  and conclusion $I && !B ==> Q$.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of s := 0 for I
part: Initialisation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  The loop is preceded by `i := 0; s := 0`, so initialisation is the
  triple
  >>> {«P»} i := 0; s := 0 {«I»}
  We compute its weakest precondition from the last statement. We
  substitute $s$ with $0$ in its postcondition, the invariant $I$.
calc:
  wp(s := 0, I)
  = (s = sum(k, 0, i - 1, a[k]) && 0 <= i <= n && n = sizeof(a))[s <- 0]
  = 0 = sum(k, 0, i - 1, a[k]) && 0 <= i <= n && n = sizeof(a)     // s is replaced by 0
set: R1 = 0 = sum(k, 0, i - 1, a[k]) && 0 <= i <= n && n = sizeof(a)

step: R2, the weakest precondition of i := 0 for R1
part: Initialisation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute $i$ with $0$ in $R1$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(i := 0, R1)
  = (0 = sum(k, 0, i - 1, a[k]) && 0 <= i <= n && n = sizeof(a))[i <- 0]
  = 0 = sum(k, 0, 0 - 1, a[k]) && 0 <= 0 <= n && n = sizeof(a)     // every i is replaced by 0
  = 0 = 0 && 0 <= 0 <= n && n = sizeof(a)     // the sum from 0 to -1 is empty, so it is 0
  = 0 <= n && n = sizeof(a)                   // 0 = 0 and 0 <= 0 are true
set: R2 = 0 <= n && n = sizeof(a)

step: P implies R2
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R2
text:
  $R2$ is the weakest precondition of `i := 0; s := 0` for $I$. By the
  consequence rule, initialisation holds when the precondition $P$
  implies $R2$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R2»
vc: n = sizeof(a) ==> 0 <= n && n = sizeof(a)
why:
  $n = sizeof(a)$ is in $P$. The size of an array is never negative, so
  $0 <= n$.

step: R3, the weakest precondition of i := i + 1 for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  One iteration must keep the invariant:
  >>> {«I» && «i < n : B»} s := s + a[i]; i := i + 1 {«I»}
  We compute the weakest precondition of the body for $I$, from the last
  statement. We substitute $i$ with $i + 1$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, I)
  = (s = sum(k, 0, i - 1, a[k]) && 0 <= i <= n && n = sizeof(a))[i <- i + 1]
  = s = sum(k, 0, i + 1 - 1, a[k]) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n && n = sizeof(a)     // every i is replaced by i + 1
  = s = sum(k, 0, i, a[k]) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n && n = sizeof(a)             // i + 1 - 1 is i
set: R3 = s = sum(k, 0, i, a[k]) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n && n = sizeof(a)

step: R4, the weakest precondition of s := s + a[i] for R3
part: Preservation
rule: ARRAY-READ, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $s$ with $s + a[i]$ in $R3$, computed in the previous
  step. The expression reads $a[i]$, so ARRAY-READ adds $def(a[i])$.
calc:
  wp(s := s + a[i], R3)
  = (s = sum(k, 0, i, a[k]) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n && n = sizeof(a))[s <- s + a[i]] && def(a[i])
  = s + a[i] = sum(k, 0, i, a[k]) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n && n = sizeof(a) && def(a[i])     // s is replaced by s + a[i]
  = s + a[i] = sum(k, 0, i, a[k]) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n && n = sizeof(a) && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)     // def(a[i]) is 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
set: R4 = s + a[i] = sum(k, 0, i, a[k]) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n && n = sizeof(a) && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)

step: I and B imply R4
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && i < n, R4
text:
  $R4$ is the weakest precondition of the body for $I$. By the consequence
  rule, preservation holds when the invariant $I$ and the loop condition
  $B$ together imply $R4$.
  >>> «I» && «i < n : B» ==> «R4»
vc: s = sum(k, 0, i - 1, a[k]) && 0 <= i <= n && n = sizeof(a) && i < n ==> s + a[i] = sum(k, 0, i, a[k]) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n && n = sizeof(a) && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
why:
  First conjunct: $sum(k, 0, i, a[k]) = sum(k, 0, i - 1, a[k]) + a[i]$,
  and $s = sum(k, 0, i - 1, a[k])$ by $I$, so the sum is $s + a[i]$.

  Bounds: $0 <= i$ gives $0 <= i + 1$, and $i < n$ gives $i + 1 <= n$.
  $n = sizeof(a)$ is in $I$.

  Definedness: $0 <= i$, and $i < n = sizeof(a)$.

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && !(i < n), Q
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and the loop condition $B$ is false. This
  must imply the postcondition $Q$.
  >>> «I» && «!(i < n) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: s = sum(k, 0, i - 1, a[k]) && 0 <= i <= n && n = sizeof(a) && !(i < n) ==> s = sum(k, 0, n - 1, a[k])
why:
  $!(i < n)$ is $i >= n$. With $i <= n$ from $I$ it gives $i = n$, so
  $s = sum(k, 0, i - 1, a[k])$ becomes $s = sum(k, 0, n - 1, a[k])$.

step: The variant is not negative
part: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL
focus: I && i < n
text:
  The counter $i$ goes up to $n$, so $n - i$ is the number of iterations
  left. We take the variant $E = n - i$. The rule WHILE-TOTAL has two
  premises. The first one: $E$ is not negative when an iteration starts.
  >>> «I» && «i < n : B» ==> «n - i >= 0 : E >= 0»
vc: s = sum(k, 0, i - 1, a[k]) && 0 <= i <= n && n = sizeof(a) && i < n ==> n - i >= 0
why:
  $i < n$ from $B$ gives $n - i > 0$.

step: The variant after i := i + 1
part: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL, ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  The second premise: the body keeps $I$ and makes $E$ smaller. $E0$ is a
  logical variable for the value of $E$ when the iteration starts.
  >>> {«I» && «i < n : B» && «n - i = E0 : E = E0»} s := s + a[i]; i := i + 1 {«I» && «n - i < E0 : E < E0»}
  Preservation already shows that the body keeps $I$, so we compute the
  weakest precondition of the body for $n - i < E0$, from the last
  statement. We substitute $i$ with $i + 1$.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, n - i < E0)
  = (n - i < E0)[i <- i + 1]
  = n - (i + 1) < E0          // i is replaced by i + 1

step: The variant after s := s + a[i]
part: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL, ARRAY-READ, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $s$ with $s + a[i]$ in the result of the previous step.
  The expression reads $a[i]$, so ARRAY-READ adds $def(a[i])$.
calc:
  wp(s := s + a[i], n - (i + 1) < E0)
  = (n - (i + 1) < E0)[s <- s + a[i]] && def(a[i])
  = n - (i + 1) < E0 && def(a[i])           // s does not occur, so nothing is replaced
  = n - (i + 1) < E0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)  // def(a[i]) is 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
text:
  The precondition of the triple must imply this formula.
vc: s = sum(k, 0, i - 1, a[k]) && 0 <= i <= n && n = sizeof(a) && i < n && n - i = E0 ==> n - (i + 1) < E0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
why:
  $n - (i + 1) = (n - i) - 1 = E0 - 1$, which is smaller than $E0$. The
  read is defined, as in preservation: $0 <= i < n = sizeof(a)$.
result: The triple is valid, and the loop terminates.
